Not in series: prepared, not applied and not installed. Installing it is decided separately,
together with the shared SDK.

The SDK's timer queue (src/core/timer_queue.cpp) waits for new timers and the next due time with
disruptorplus' spin_wait_strategy: its dispatch thread polls and took about 20 % of a core in a
native profile (docs/performance-profile.md), because the kernel's 1 ms KeTimeStampBundle timer
(xboxkrnl_module.cpp) is always queued. This patch makes it wait on a condition variable
(blocking_wait_strategy), and fixes that strategy's timed waits, which passed the predicate before
the timeout to std::condition_variable::wait_for/wait_until and never compiled (nothing used them).

The queue runs the POSIX guest timers (threading_posix.cpp: KeSetTimer and the waitable timers) and
the KeTimeStampBundle tick on every platform, so a blocking wait can change their precision. A
standalone check of the fixed strategy on Linux, 2000 deadlines 1 ms apart: late by 58 us median,
149 us p99, 194 us max (the spin was late by about nothing). On Windows the wait goes through
SleepConditionVariableSRW in whole milliseconds, with the 1 ms timer resolution of
rexglue-win-timer-resolution.patch. When measuring it, besides the CPU of the
rex::thread::TimerQueue thread, compare the frame rate and the frame times (mean, p95, p99, frames
over 33 and 50 ms) and the vblank pacing against the unpatched SDK, on Linux and on Windows.

